An [image-set()](CSS-IMAGES-4#funcdef-image-set) function contains a list of one or more [\<image-set-option>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image-set-option)s, and must select only one of them to determine what image it will represent:

1.  First, remove any [\<image-set-option>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image-set-option)s from the list that specify an unknown or unsupported MIME type in their [type()](CSS-IMAGES-4#funcdef-image-set-type) value.

2.  Second, remove any [\<image-set-option>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image-set-option)s from the list that have the same [\<resolution>](CSS-VALUES-4#resolution-value) as a previous option in the list.

3.  If there are no [\<image-set-option>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image-set-option) left at this point, the function represents an [invalid image](CSS-IMAGES-4#invalid-image).

4.  Finally, among the remaining [\<image-set-option>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image-set-option)s, make a UA-specific choice of which to load, based on whatever criteria deemed relevant (such as the resolution of the display, connection speed, etc).

5.  The [image-set()](CSS-IMAGES-4#funcdef-image-set) function then represents the [\<image>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image) of the chosen [\<image-set-option>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image-set-option).

UAs **may** change which [\<image-set-option>](CSS-IMAGES-4#typedef-image-set-option) they wish to use for a given [image-set()](CSS-IMAGES-4#funcdef-image-set) over the lifetime of the page, if the criteria used to determine which option to choose change significantly enough to make it worthwhile in the UA’s estimation.

> **Example:** This example shows how to use [image-set()](CSS-IMAGES-4#funcdef-image-set) to provide an image in three versions: a "normal" version, a "high-res" version, and an extra-high resolution version for use in high-quality printing (as printers can have *extremely* high resolution):
>
> ```
> background-image: image-set( "foo.png" 1x,
>                              "foo-2x.png" 2x,
>                              "foo-print.png" 600dpi );
> ```

> **Example:** This example shows use of the [type()](CSS-IMAGES-4#funcdef-image-set-type) function to serve multiple versions of the same image in both new, higher-quality formats, and older, more widely-supported formats:
>
> ```
> background-image: image-set( "foo.avif" type("image/avif"),
>                              "foo.jpg" type("image/jpeg") );
> ```
>
> Note that the AVIF image is given first; since both images have the same resolution (defaulting to 1x since it’s unspecified), the JPEG image, coming second, is automatically dropped in UAs that support AVIF images.
>
> In older UAs, however, the AVIF image is ignored (because the UA knows it doesn’t support `"image/avif"` files), and so the JPEG is chosen instead.

> **Example:** Raster images can be mixed with vector images, or even CSS generated images.For example, in this code snippet a high-resolution image with subtle details is used on screens that can do it justice, while an ordinary CSS [linear-gradient()](CSS-IMAGES-3#funcdef-linear-gradient) is used instead for low-resolution situations:
>
> ```
> background-image: image-set( linear-gradient(cornflowerblue, white) 1x,
>                              url("detailed-gradient.png") 3x );
> ```

